> > B2: Explain the data structure used to track priority donation.
> > Use ASCII art to diagram a nested donation. (Alternately, submit a
> > .png file.)

Each thread contains a donor_list. When a higher-priority thread waits for a lock held by a lower-priority thread, the waiting thread is inserted into the lock holder's donor_list using donor_elem. The waiting thread also stores the lock in desired_lock. old_priority preserves the holder's base priority.

For a nested donation:

```txt
	High, priority 40
		|
		| waits for lock B
		v
	Medium, priority 30, holds B
		|
		| waits for lock A
		v
	Low, priority 20, holds A
```
High donates priority 40 to Medium. Because Medium is waiting for a lock held by Low, the donation propagates recursively to Low:
```txt
  High(40) -> Medium(40) -> Low(40)
```

---- ALGORITHMS ----

> > B3: How do you ensure that the highest priority thread waiting for
> > a lock, semaphore, or condition variable wakes up first?

The ready list is maintained in descending priority order using
list_insert_ordered(). When a semaphore wakes a thread, sema_up() sorts its
waiter list and removes the first element. Condition-variable waiters store
their priorities in semaphore_elem, and cond_signal() sorts that list before
waking its first waiter. Locks use semaphores internally, so lock waiters are
also awakened in priority order.

> > B4: Describe the sequence of events when a call to lock_acquire()
> > causes a priority donation. How is nested donation handled?|

If the holder is itself waiting for another lock, donation_helper() recursively
propagates the effective priority through the chain of lock holders. The
current thread then blocks in sema_down() until it can acquire the lock.

> > B5: Describe the sequence of events when lock_release() is called
> > on a lock that a higher-priority thread is waiting for.

When lock_release() is called, the current thread removes donations made by
threads waiting for that specific lock. It restores its effective priority
to old_priority, then scans its remaining donor_list and reapplies the highest
remaining donation. Finally, it clears the lock holder and calls sema_up(),
which wakes the highest-priority waiter. If that waiter has a higher priority,
the releasing thread yields.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

> > B6: Describe a potential race in thread_set_priority() and explain
> > how your implementation avoids it. Can you use a lock to avoid
> > this race?

Our implementation disables interrupts while updating old_priority and
priority. This makes the change atomic on Pintos's single processor. After
interrupts are restored, yield_higher_priority() checks whether the running
thread should yield.

A lock is not appropriate here. Acquiring a lock can block and invoke the
scheduler, but the scheduler data must already be protected while priorities
are being changed. Disabling interrupts avoids that circular dependency.


---- RATIONALE ----

> > B7: Why did you choose this design? In what ways is it superior to
> > another design you considered?

We chose ordered lists because they fit the existing Pintos list API and keep
selection of the next runnable or waiting thread simple: the highest-priority
thread is at the front. Donation metadata is stored in struct thread because
donations are relationships between waiting threads and lock holders.

The main disadvantage is that insertion and re-sorting take linear or
O(n log n) time. A more scalable scheduler could maintain separate queues for
each priority. For this project, ordered lists are simpler and sufficient for
the expected number of threads.

